e number of marbles. A total of $63%$ of Matt's marbles are blue, while $\\frac{3}{4}$ of Abraham's marbles are blue. Select the options that make the statements below true.\n\nA total of\n{{field-1}}\nof Abraham's marbles are blue.\n\nTherefore, he has\n{{field-2}}\nblue marbles compared to Matt." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147799 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57efd73d-0f59-56f1-a156-7f1ee9e1fa2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147801 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147801 #edb/ref 1069 "q-348980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147801 #edb/ref 1071 "Lionel and David are youth soccer players. In the previous season, $12%$ of Lionel’s goals were scored from the penalty spot, while the decimal proportion of David’s goals scored from the penalty spot was $0.1$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Less than half of Lionel's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n2. More than half of David's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n3. Lionel had a greater percentage of goals scored from the penalty spot compared to David." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147801 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "436201f3-78ea-5cda-b175-c5331e2f77ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148482 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148482 #edb/ref 1061 632742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148482 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9c7592c-10c0-5cc3-b1ff-b5b67dfe08ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148482 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148483 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148483 #edb/ref 1061 632743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148483 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5df02fb6-3576-5996-8b1e-64978fa7b5d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148483 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148484 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148484 #edb/ref 1061 632746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148484 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6835341-5e27-57e3-ae67-a97b6df1ba75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148484 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148485 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148485 #edb/ref 1061 632749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148485 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4ce04142-5c86-5b4c-a76f-55af88901484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148485 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1061 632750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0e51654-69d7-5097-b27f-71f3c28bce97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148486 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1061 632753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99330381-c770-5a62-a3c7-a79ba13fcd0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148487 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1061 632754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5102e680-a474-5349-a6c9-27bc67303b7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose Ethan and Ryan are baseball players. In the previous season, $24\\%$ of Ethan’s hits were home runs, while the decimal proportion of Ryan’s hits that were home runs was $0.3$.\n\nLet’s determine whether either Ethan or Ryan hit home runs on more than half of their total hits.\n\n- *For Ethan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a percentage, $50\\%$. Since\n$24\\% < 50\\%$,\nless than half of Ethan’s hits were home runs.\n- *For Ryan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a decimal, $0.5$. Since\n$0.3 < 0.5$,\nless than half of Ryan’s hits were home runs.\n\nTherefore, neither player hit home runs on more than half of their hits.\n\nNow, let’s see more concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1065 14935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fbb4d977-7db5-579e-baf0-bb8e13b6c15a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148670 #edb/ref 1067 "Comparing Decimals and Percents in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose Carlos and Henry have the same number of trading cards. A total of $32 \\%$ of Carlos's cards are rare cards, while\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nof Henry's cards are rare cards.\n\nWho has more rare cards?\n\nTo answer this, we need to *compare the fraction and the percentage*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 32 \\%\n$$\n\nA common method is to convert the fraction to a percentage.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\dfrac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100} = 25 \\%\n$$\n\nSince $25 \\% < 32 \\%$, we can conclude that Carlos has *more* rare cards than Carlos." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1065 14937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4be68676-8fcc-56ce-a8ef-96b2a954a03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148671 #edb/ref 1067 "Comparing Fractions and Percents in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1064 "When working with fractions, percents, and decimals, we often need to decide which number is greater or smaller. Since these numbers are written in different forms, it can be difficult to compare them directly.\n\nTo make comparisons easier, we can *convert them to a common form*.\n\nFor example, let's compare the percent $48\\%$ and the decimal $0.52$.\n\nOne way to do that is to convert the percent to a decimal. So, we divide the percent value by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n48 \\div 100 = 0.48\n$$\n\nThus, $48\\%$ is equivalent to $0.48$.\n\nNow, we can compare the two decimals:\n$0.48 < 0.52$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $48\\%$ is smaller than $0.52$.\n\nLet's see more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1065 2078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e6681afa-a432-5074-a46c-204561f2cf65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148698 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
